1
00:00:00,420 --> 00:00:07,589
In this Day Two exercise, you will teach QuPath
to recognise tissue by painting examples on a
skin image.

2
00:00:07,819 --> 00:00:13,603
We will start with three practice classes:
epidermis, dermis, and whitespace.

3
00:00:13,833 --> 00:00:18,362
Then we will check the prediction and convert
it into annotations you can measure.

4
00:00:19,503 --> 00:00:26,488
Create a working project, add skin.ome.tif, and
open it from the Project tab.

5
00:00:26,718 --> 00:00:34,567
Set the image type to Brightfield (H&E). Zoom
into an edge where you can see purple
epidermis, pink dermis, and empty glass.

6
00:00:34,797 --> 00:00:41,621
For this warm-up, use the + button in the class
list to add Epidermis, Dermis, and Whitespace.

7
00:00:41,851 --> 00:00:50,550
For the full six-layer exercise, run the course
setup script before you start painting. It
clears earlier annotations.

8
00:00:51,670 --> 00:01:01,544
Open the Annotations tab. Choose Epidermis in
the class list and turn on Auto set. New
strokes will then inherit the highlighted
class.

9
00:01:01,774 --> 00:01:09,938
Select the Brush in the toolbar, or press B
with the viewer in focus. The circle shows the
area the brush will paint.

10
00:01:11,045 --> 00:01:18,659
Place the brush inside a clear patch of
epidermis. Press, drag a short distance, and
release.

11
00:01:18,889 --> 00:01:28,583
Check that the new annotation is labelled
Epidermis. With the annotation selected, hold
Shift to add to it, or Alt to erase with the
brush.

12
00:01:28,813 --> 00:01:35,442
Switch the class to Dermis before painting a
pink patch. Use Whitespace for empty glass.

13
00:01:35,672 --> 00:01:44,071
Keep each stroke inside one class. A few small
examples from different areas give the
classifier more useful variety.

14
00:01:45,212 --> 00:01:51,066
Open Classify, Pixel classification, then Train
pixel classifier.

15
00:01:51,296 --> 00:01:59,655
Start with Random Trees, Moderate resolution,
and Classification output. Leave the default
features for your first run.

16
00:01:59,885 --> 00:02:06,439
Click Live prediction. The coloured overlay
shows the class QuPath predicts for each pixel.

17
00:02:07,545 --> 00:02:16,559
Compare the first prediction with the
underlying image. In this example, some dark
areas within the dermis are predicted as
epidermis.

18
00:02:16,789 --> 00:02:25,538
Add small, correctly labelled examples that
cover this variation. Keeping Live prediction
on lets you see the updated result.

19
00:02:25,768 --> 00:02:33,592
Compare an unlabelled region again. Press C to
toggle the classification overlay and inspect
the tissue underneath.

20
00:02:34,712 --> 00:02:41,756
Name and save the classifier. Preserve your
training examples separately before measuring
the predictions.

21
00:02:41,986 --> 00:02:50,420
Open the reporting image and use Load pixel
classifier to select your saved model. Draw and
select the region you want to measure.

22
00:02:50,650 --> 00:02:58,904
Click Create objects. Choose Current selection
for a selected region, or Full image to process
the entire image.

23
00:02:59,134 --> 00:03:05,728
Set the new object type to Annotation. Now the
predictions have outlines and can be measured.

24
00:03:06,837 --> 00:03:13,986
Open Show annotation measurements. The area is
calculated from the outline and the image
calibration.

25
00:03:14,216 --> 00:03:23,205
Compare each outline with the original image.
Save the new annotations and record the
classifier, resolution, and analysis region.

26
00:03:24,337 --> 00:03:29,741
Repeat the workflow for the full lesson using
its six tissue classes and Whitespace.

27
00:03:29,971 --> 00:03:40,855
After Create objects, run the course report
script. Its percentages use the total area of
the six tissue classes. Whitespace is excluded.

28
00:03:41,085 --> 00:03:47,924
The sequence is the same: paint examples, check
the prediction, create annotations, and
measure.
